# Spawn Remote Coding Session

## Overview

Launch one isolated remote coding session by invoking the existing launcher. The
agent is **Claude Code by default**; Codex and Cursor are used only when the user
asks for one. The launcher owns validation, unique naming, background tmux
startup, verification, and failed-launch cleanup.

The three agents reach remote clients differently, and that difference is visible
in what the launcher reports:

- **Claude Code** registers Remote Control per session, so each session shows up
  on its own at claude.ai/code and in the phone app under its display name.
  Claude Code creates and cleans up its own worktree.
- **Cursor** registers a named private worker with Cursor, scoped to the
  directories it is given. The launcher creates the worktree, starts the worker,
  and reports a `cursor.com/agents#workerId=…` link for that session. No agent
  runs locally: the user starts an agent against the worker from Cursor on the
  web or their phone.
- **Codex** has no per-session remote flag. Remote control is machine-wide: one
  `codex remote-control` daemon carries every thread on this host, and threads
  are opened from the ChatGPT app by picking a directory. The launcher therefore
  ensures the daemon is running and enrolled, creates the Git worktree itself,
  and starts a Codex session in it. The display name is used for local resource
  names only — Codex names its own threads.

## Workflow

1. Choose the agent.
   - Default to `claude` whenever the request does not name an agent.
   - Use `codex` only when the user explicitly asks for Codex, OpenAI's coding
     agent, or GPT-based coding. Phrases like "codex session", "use codex",
     "spawn this on codex", or "an OpenAI agent instead" select Codex.
   - Use `cursor` only when the user explicitly asks for Cursor, the Cursor
     agent, or a Cursor worker. Phrases like "cursor session", "use cursor", or
     "spawn this on cursor" select Cursor.
   - Never infer Codex or Cursor from the subject matter of the work.
   - If the user asks for more than one agent on the same project, that is one
     launcher call per agent.

2. Extract the main project and any additional projects from the request.
   - Accept only a direct child name beneath `${WORKSPACE_DIR}`.
   - Known projects are `AppNavigationMacros`, `ServerIdentifiableMacros`,
     `artemis`, `backend`, `fluttershy`, and `iphone`.
   - `iphone` may still be cloning; invoke the launcher normally and report its
     validation error if it is not yet a usable Git repository.
   - Require the pattern `^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*$`.
   - The main project is the project described as main, primary, or first (for
     example, `artemis` in "an artemis session along with backend"). It receives
     the worktree.
   - Pass every other requested project as an additional project. Every agent
     exposes these as extra workspace directories; they do not receive worktrees.
   - Preserve each project's exact on-disk capitalization.
   - Remove duplicate project mentions while preserving order.
   - If the main project is missing or ambiguous, ask the user for it and stop.
   - Never pass a path, slash, shell operator, or untrusted fragment as the
     project argument.

3. Choose a useful human-facing session display name.
   - Use the user's requested name when it matches the safe-name pattern.
   - Otherwise infer a concise lowercase kebab-case name from the work requested
     and project context, ideally 2–5 words. Examples: `artemis-login-fix`,
     `ios-navigation-cleanup`, or `cross-project-auth`.
   - Include the main project when it improves recognition, but avoid
     mechanically listing every added project.
   - If the user provides no task context, use `<main-project>-session` for one
     project or `<main-project>-multi-project` when additional projects are
     present.
   - Never use the generic name `hermes`.
   - Never add a timestamp, PID, random suffix, or uniqueness token. The launcher
     keeps the display name readable and separately generates unique internal
     worktree and tmux names.

4. Invoke the `terminal` tool with exactly one of these command shapes:

   ```bash
   ${HOME}/spawn-session.sh <main-project> <display-name>
   ${HOME}/spawn-session.sh <main-project> <display-name> <additional-project> [additional-project ...]
   ${HOME}/spawn-session.sh --agent codex <main-project> <display-name>
   ${HOME}/spawn-session.sh --agent codex <main-project> <display-name> <additional-project> [additional-project ...]
   ${HOME}/spawn-session.sh --agent cursor <main-project> <display-name>
   ${HOME}/spawn-session.sh --agent cursor <main-project> <display-name> <additional-project> [additional-project ...]
   ```

   Run it in the foreground and wait for completion. `--agent`, when present,
   comes first; omit it for Claude Code rather than passing `--agent claude`.
   Put the main project next, followed by the display name, then additional
   projects in the order requested. Do not `cd`, call `claude`, `codex`, or
   `cursor-agent` directly, create a worktree manually, wrap the command in
   `nohup`, or add shell pipelines. A Cursor launch waits on a network
   registration round trip, so give it time rather than assuming it hung.

5. Use a real tool call.
   - Invoke the terminal tool; never print a JSON description of a proposed tool
     call in chat.
   - Treat terminal output as the source of truth.

6. Report the result.
   - Claim success only when the command exits successfully and prints
     `Remote session is running.`
   - Include the agent, the session name or label, the tmux session name, and
     the worktree shown by the launcher.
   - For Cursor, quote the `Run agents:` link verbatim and say that an agent has
     to be started against the worker from Cursor before any work happens.
   - For Codex, also pass along how to reach the session: the host is picked in
     the ChatGPT app and the thread is opened in the worktree path the launcher
     printed.
   - On failure, quote the concise error and stop. Do not improvise a different
     launch procedure.
   - A Codex launch fails before creating anything when this machine's Codex
     remote control is not enrolled — for example a workspace-access error from
     `codex remote-control start`. Report that error as-is; it needs a ChatGPT
     workspace or account change, not a different launcher invocation.
   - A Cursor launch fails the same way when the Cursor CLI is signed out; the
     fix is `cursor-agent login`, which the user runs themselves.
   - Never silently fall back to a different agent than the one requested.

## Examples

User: `Start an Artemis Claude session.`

Terminal command:

```bash
${HOME}/spawn-session.sh artemis artemis-session
```

User: `Launch a remote session for iphone called login-fix.`

Terminal command:

```bash
${HOME}/spawn-session.sh iphone login-fix
```

User: `Start a new artemis session along with backend, iphone, and fluttershy.`

Terminal command:

```bash
${HOME}/spawn-session.sh artemis artemis-multi-project backend iphone fluttershy
```

User: `Spin up a codex session on backend to clean up the migrations.`

Terminal command:

```bash
${HOME}/spawn-session.sh --agent codex backend backend-migration-cleanup
```

User: `Use Codex for an artemis session that can also see backend.`

Terminal command:

```bash
${HOME}/spawn-session.sh --agent codex artemis artemis-codex-session backend
```

User: `Start a cursor session on fluttershy for the onboarding rewrite.`

Terminal command:

```bash
${HOME}/spawn-session.sh --agent cursor fluttershy fluttershy-onboarding-rewrite
```

## Common Pitfalls

1. **Displaying tool JSON instead of executing it:** invoke `terminal`; prose
   does not launch anything.
2. **Reaching for Codex or Cursor unprompted:** Claude Code is the default. Only
   an explicit request selects another agent.
3. **Starting the agent separately:** the launcher already starts it in tmux, and
   already starts the Codex remote-control daemon or the Cursor worker when one
   is needed.
4. **Manufacturing uniqueness:** keep the display name readable; the launcher
   creates separate unique internal identifiers.
5. **Creating worktrees for added directories:** only the main project receives a
   worktree; added projects are shared directories.
6. **Promising a named Codex thread:** the display name names local resources
   only. Do not tell the user to look for it by name in the ChatGPT app. The
   Cursor worker name, by contrast, is exactly what appears in Cursor.
7. **Reporting a Cursor session as already working:** the launcher registers the
   worker; the agent still has to be started from Cursor.
8. **Launching once per project:** a multi-project request is one combined
   session and one launcher call unless the user explicitly asks for separate
   sessions.
9. **Reporting before completion:** wait for the launcher exit and use its actual
   output.
10. **Trying an alternative after failure:** preserve the launcher's validation
   and cleanup guarantees; report the error.

## Verification Checklist

- [ ] The agent matches the request, defaulting to Claude Code.
- [ ] Main project, additional projects, and display name match the safe-name
      pattern.
- [ ] The display name reflects the requested work and contains no timestamp or
      PID.
- [ ] `--agent codex` or `--agent cursor` appears only when the user asked for
      that agent, and appears before the project arguments.
- [ ] The intended main project is the first positional launcher argument.
- [ ] Exactly one launcher command was executed through `terminal`.
- [ ] The command completed with a successful exit status.
- [ ] The response reports actual launcher output rather than intended actions.
